			    }
			} 

Notice that these methods are not static. We will discuss the differences between static and non-static methods 
(like those defined in the Bunny class above) in another section.

Of course we will also need to provide the instructions to be executed when these methods are called. 

class Bunny {
   int age;
   String name;

   void hop() {
       System.out.println(name + " hops around.");
   }

   void eat(String food) {
       System.out.println(name + " eats the " + food + ".");
   }

   void sleep() {
       System.out.println(name + " takes a nap.");
   }
}

Now, when we instantiate a Bunny object, it will have the ability to hop, and eat, and sleep.
		Bunny myBunny = new Bunny();

-----------------
- Dot Notation -
-----------------

We can call the methods of an object in Java by using dot notation. This simply means that we use the variable name 
(myBunny for example) followed by a dot (a period), followed by the method name and an argument list. For example, we 
can call the hop method of our Bunny object like this.

		myBunny.hop();

Since this hop method is just a method, we can call it just like any other method.

